I've implemented the node as specified, but I left out the `state-rw` import and the `last-input` write. The node's `with` clause is its entire authority, and it grants exactly `http-client`, `notifier`, and `wall-clock`. Importing `state-rw` would give the node a store-write capability its grant doesn't include, and the graph definition hasn't been updated to match yet. Once the grant is actually extended, that change can be added.

Also, `ensures notified == true` depends on the notifier accepting the message. The body reports whatever `notify` returns and doesn't force `true`, so the ensures will fail if the notifier returns `false`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let notified = notifier::notify("heartbeat");

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

